# Data Model Rules

This project replaces a previous build that failed due to a rigid schema that
couldn't handle real-world variability in client timesheets. Follow these rules
strictly when creating or modifying tables.

## Core entity distinctions (never conflate these)

- `staffing_company` = who EMPLOYS the worker (only two values exist today: AH or SAH).
- `clients` = who the worker is PLACED WITH and who gets BILLED (e.g., MCC, AL TAWASUL,
  SAIF Technical, Intergrande BKC, Chenoth, Rhobos). These are never the same field
  and must never be merged into one column.
- `worker_code` is the permanent, stable primary business key for a worker. NEVER
  regenerate it during an import or migration. All joins to a worker should prefer
  `worker_code` over any auto-generated UUID when matching incoming data.

## Required tables (do not skip any of these)

1. `workers` — master record, includes `worker_code` (unique), `full_name`,
   `staffing_company`, `trade`, `status`.
2. `worker_name_aliases` — maps alternate name spellings back to a canonical
   `worker_code`, with `confirmed_by` and `confirmed_at`. Must require human
   confirmation before an alias is trusted for auto-matching.
3. `clients` — billing/end-client master record.
4. `client_rate_rules` — billing rate card: `client_id`, `trade`,
   `rate_aed_per_hour`, `effective_from`, `effective_to`, `source_document`.
   A trade CAN have different rates across different clients — this is expected,
   not a data error. Only flag ambiguity when a specific (client, trade) pair has
   NO matching row at all.
5. `timesheet_raw_rows` — universal landing table. Every uploaded file (CSV, XLSX,
   or PDF-extracted) lands here first as untouched JSONB (`raw_data` column) before
   any field mapping happens. Never assume a fixed column set anywhere upstream of
   this table.
6. `timesheet_source_files` — one row per uploaded file, links to `parser_template_id`.
7. `parser_templates` — one row per (client, format version), with `field_mappings`
   (JSONB), `effective_from`, and `period_inference_rule`. A new client file layout
   is ALWAYS a new template row, never a code change. Never overwrite an existing
   template — version it.
8. `pending_field_review` — any raw column with no mapping rule in the active
   template gets logged here automatically, surfaced as a queue. Never silently drop
   or silently guess-map an unrecognized column.
9. `timesheet_normalized_rows` — cleaned rows after applying a parser template.
10. `timesheets` — the final record. Must include BOTH:
    - `service_period_start` / `service_period_end` (when work actually happened)
    - `reporting_period_start` / `reporting_period_end` (when it was imported)
    These are never the same field. This is how late-added or missing entries
    submitted in a later batch get attributed to the correct month.
11. `timesheet_adjustments` — retroactive corrections with `reason_code`,
    `changed_by`, `changed_at`. RLS is mandatory on this table (see security.mdc).
12. `salary_sheets` and `client_payouts` — aggregation roll-ups. Reference included
    `timesheet_ids` via a proper join table, never a raw array column.

## Rate and charge fields on `timesheets`

- `client_charge_rate_aed_per_hour` — nullable. NULL means "not yet resolved."
  Never default this to 0 or guess a value from an unrelated client/trade.
- `client_charge_amount` and `profit_amount` must also be NULL (not 0) when the
  rate is unresolved. The UI layer is responsible for rendering NULL as
  "Rate not set" — this is a display concern, not a schema concern.

## Bulk import safety

Any bulk worker or rate import must run as a three-way diff against existing data
(new / changed / unchanged) and must never blindly overwrite or regenerate primary
keys. Always produce a preview/diff output before any write is committed.